    25	## In plain words
    26	
    27	This amendment has two parts.
    28	
    29	**Part 1** corrects six wording problems in Amendment 9. The owner decided these corrections. They add no
    30	mechanism. The verifier uses the same checks and gives the same verdicts. A review of the plain version of
    31	Amendment 9 found the six problems.
    32	
    33	Bitcoin is a public system that adds blocks to a chain. A block header states a chain time. This value is not a
    34	measurement of real time. The registered number k is six.
    35	
    36	- **A01.** Amendment 9 says that the content existed "no later than" T. T is a chain time, not real time. The
    37	  correction says this in the claim and in the other sentences that use T in this way.
    38	- **A02.** Amendment 9 says, in its own words, that the keys "were allowed to issue during a stated window". The
    39	  verifier makes only two comparisons with the window. The correction states only those two comparisons.
    40	- **A03.** Amendment 9 excludes Bitcoin history "deeper than k". The correction defines the depth of a
    41	  reorganization. The exclusion then also includes a reorganization of exactly k blocks. The models already
    42	  exclude that case.
    43	- **A04.** Amendment 9 does not say which receipts V1 protects after an attacker obtains authority-record
    44	  credentials. The author must decide this item. Part 2 adds one more option for it.
    45	  - The recommended text protects the frames and signatures that the service keys made honestly. This protection
    46	    applies before and after the attacker obtains the credentials.
    47	  - Without the key list of Part 2, V1 does not prevent new receipts under the attacker's own keys.
    48	  - The service must publish its authority record. A third party can inspect a substituted key if that record is
    49	    public. The verifier does not check publication. V1 does not guarantee that an attacker publishes the record.
    50	- **A05.** Amendment 9 does not give the order of the steps that select a Bitcoin attestation. The correction
    51	  gives the steps that the profile already uses.
    52	- **A06.** DNS (the Domain Name System) holds one authority record. Amendment 9 says "owner name D". The verifier
    53	  must check the DNS owner name `_tessera-v1.wamason.com`. The correction names it.
    54	
    55	**Part 2** has three new items. The owner and Codex recommend them. Codex is a second AI system that reviews this
    56	work. The author has not decided these items. If the author accepts them, V1 gets new mechanism.
    57	
    58	**UTC: what U is.** UTC (Coordinated Universal Time) is the standard measure of real time. Amendment 9 states no
    59	UTC time.
    60	
    61	- Under A-UTC, U is a UTC upper bound: the latest chain time of the six confirming blocks, plus two hours.
    62	- The claim is conditional: if A-UTC is true, the content and both signatures existed before U.
    63	
    64	**UTC: the assumption A-UTC.** A miner is a party that makes Bitcoin blocks.
    65	
    66	- A-UTC says: at least one of those six blocks states a chain time no more than two hours before its real
    67	  production time.
    68	- A-UTC is false only if the miners of all six blocks state chain times more than two hours early.
    69	- Nothing in Bitcoin or in the verifier makes sure that A-UTC is true.
    70	- The author's signature accepts A-UTC as an assumption. The signature does not show that miners make A-UTC true.
    71	
    72	**UTC: the evidence.**
    73	
    74	- The retained measurements cover 2015 to 2026. In each complete set of six, one recorded arrival was no later
    75	  than chain time plus 1065 seconds.
    76	- The dataset rejects arrival times more than two hours from the chain time. It cannot show failures beyond that
    77	  limit.
    78	- Observer clocks can also be wrong. These measurements do not show that A-UTC was true.
    79	
    80	**UTC: the output.**
    81	
    82	- The verifier reports U with its assumption identifier, `A-UTC-7200-6`, in `utc_assessment`.
    83	- U, conditional on A-UTC, is outside the six fields that later verifiers must reproduce. U does not change the
    84	  verdict.
    85	- Each statement of U must give the assumption A-UTC next to it.
    86	
    87	**UTC: the year 2106.** In 2106 the Bitcoin time field reaches its maximum. The calculation of U, whose UTC claim
    88	depends on A-UTC, stays correct for the saved headers of older blocks. Bitcoin, not V1, must define the times of
    89	blocks after that point.
    90	
    91	**Key substitution: the problem.** An attacker with both authority-record credentials can make authority records
    92	that name the attacker's own keys. Without a new mechanism, receipts under those keys can get VALID.
    93	
    94	**Key substitution: option (e).**
    95	
    96	- Option (e) adds a list of the service keys to the trust file. The release key authenticates the trust file.
    97	- The verifier gives INVALID or CANNOT VERIFY for a receipt whose keys are not on the list.
    98	- The release-key holder must not add keys to the list only because the two authority records name them.
    99	- The author can also choose option (a), the earlier recommendation without the list, or options (b) to (d).
   100	
   101	**Key substitution: the limits.**
   102	
   103	- The protection applies only to a user whose verifier release is authentic and comes from an honest release-key
   104	  holder.
   105	- It stops those users from accepting such receipts.
   106	- It does not make sure that anybody finds or publishes the attack.
   107	- An attacker who also has the release key can make a release that lists the attacker's keys.
   108	
   109	**Release-key custody: the rule.** AWS means Amazon Web Services, the provider of the service's cloud account.
   110	Amendment 9 already requires these access limits:
   111	
   112	- The DNS credential and the repository key must not give access to the release key.
   113	- Access to the AWS account must not give access to the release key.
   114	- Hardware or offline software for the release key is acceptable only with a written procedure.
   115	
   116	The procedure has these contents:
   117	
   118	- The procedure must say who authorizes each release.
   119	- The procedure must say where the signing occurs.
   120	- The procedure must say how backups stay outside the systems that operate the service.
   121	- The procedure must say how a separate system checks each release before the signing.
   122	- The procedure must say how files move into and out of the signing system.
   123	
   124	**Release-key custody: when.**
   125	
   126	- At the freeze, the rule, the release format and the contents of the procedure must be fixed.
   127	- After the freeze, the owner builds and tests the signing system.
   128	- A reviewer must check the procedure before the first public verifier release.
   129	- The target for that release is before 20 November 2026.
   130	
   131	**Release-key custody: test releases.** Each release before the first public verifier release must use a separate
   132	test release key. Users never get the test release key as a release key. So no user can take an early release as a
   133	real release.
   134	
   135	**Release-key custody: risks that the owner names.**
   136	
   137	- The schedule risk moves later. The owner must start the preparation early.
   138	- The rules do not change after the freeze, because the release format is fixed at the freeze.
   139	- The test-key rule stops the use of an early release as a real release.
   140	- The author accepts the use of his time in November. He wrote: "(4) is acceptable to me".
   141	- No user is at risk from this change, because nothing is public before the check.
   142	
   143	**Release-key custody: if independence fails.** The purchase of hardware alone does not make the key independent.
   144	If the owner cannot show independence before the first public verifier release, the author gets a NARROW-MODEL. That
   145	model is a smaller protection against an attacker with authority-record credentials. The removal of the key list
   146	does not repair this.
   147	
   148	Two other mechanisms are recorded as candidates only: time-stamp authorities and a witnessed public log. This
   149	amendment does not require anybody to make them.
   150	
   151	Each change has a kind:
   152	
   153	- CLARIFY: the meaning does not change.
   154	- NARROW-CLAIM: the claim becomes smaller, so that it states only what the checks establish.
   155	- CORRECT: the wording disagreed with a registered rule or model. The correction follows that rule or model
   156	  without reducing the claim.
   157	- BROADEN: the claim becomes larger. It adds a mechanism or a stated assumption.
   158	- GATE: the change adds or changes a prerequisite, a trigger, or a cut order. The row states any scope change that
   159	  follows.
   160	
   161	New words in this summary (owner proposals; not yet in the plain-language dictionary):
   162	
   163	| Word | Meaning |
   164	|---|---|
   165	| UTC | Coordinated Universal Time, the standard measure of real time. |
   166	| miner | A party that makes Bitcoin blocks. |
   167	| U | Under A-UTC, a UTC upper bound: the latest chain time of the six confirming blocks, plus two hours. |
   168	| A-UTC | The assumption that at least one of those six blocks states a chain time no more than two hours before its real production time. |
   169	| key list | The list of the service keys in the trust file. |
   170	| freeze | The point, by 30 October 2026, after which the V1 rules do not change. |
   171	| first public verifier release | The first verifier release that users get, signed with the real release key. |
   172	| test release key | A separate key that signs each verifier release before the first public verifier release. Users never accept it as the release key. |
   173	| NARROW-MODEL | A smaller model of the attacker against whom V1 holds. Only the author can accept it. |
