   206	
   207	## A10.1 "Existed no later than T" is chain time, not UTC — OWNER DECISION, NARROW-CLAIM (review A01)
   208	
   209	A9 says the bytes and signatures "existed no later than a time T fixed by Bitcoin" (A9:36-37; again at :114,
   210	:133 and in C5, :144). A9 also says that header times are chain-time proxies (C5) and that a finite UTC cutoff
   211	"requires an additional, explicit backward-skew assumption that V1 does not establish" (:444-446). The ordinary
   212	reading of "no later than T" is a real-time bound, which A9 does not support.
   213	
   214	**Reading adopted.** "No later than T" means chain order: the bytes and exact signatures existed before the
   215	selected Bitcoin block was mined, because that block commits to their SHA-256 hash (under the registered
   216	cryptographic and chain assumptions). T is that block's header time, a chain-time value. It is not an upper bound
   217	in UTC: A2.1 registers no finite bound on B − C, and neither ε nor S is one. A UTC reading needs the additional
   218	backward-skew assumption that V1 does not establish. A10.1.1 recommends one such assumption, for a separate
   219	assessment only; this section does not depend on it.
   220	
   221	**Other registered uses of the same bound.** The scenario mapping (:177, :186), the issue order (:294-295) and
   222	guarantee 1 (:433-439) use T as the same clock bound. Each is qualified the same way (rows 5–9 of A10.7). These
   223	qualifications change the explanation of the safe decisions, not their verdicts, fixtures or case membership.
   224	Scenario i's parties' signature-checking duty and guarantee 1's `historical_security: NOT_EVALUATED` stay. The
   225	issue-order case is concrete: a signature made after a backward-skewed header's nominal UTC time, but before the
   226	block is mined, can fit the block's anchor.
   227	
   228	**Assembled C5 claim cell** (rows 4 and 17 of A10.7 both point here):
   229	
   230	> The frames **and their exact signatures** existed before the selected Bitcoin block was mined. T is that block's
   231	> header time, not a UTC upper bound. The selected block belongs to the qualifying Bitcoin attestation at the lowest
   232	> block height in the presented OTS proof, under A10.5's stages; T and its confirmations come from that same
   233	> attestation. The three-conjunct predicate holds at k = 6. Header times need not be monotone; selection uses chain
   234	> order, not the least header time (OWNER DECISION 2026-10-14, F03).
   235	
   236	C5's non-claims stay, with the reorganization residual amended by A10.3 (row 14). If the author adopts A10.1.1,
   237	row 21 adds its conditional sentence after this cell; the cell itself does not change.
   238	
   239	**A1.6.** A1.6 (:485-505) contains older, compressed clock and depth language. A1 stays standing. This amendment is
   240	a V1 qualification of that inherited clock wording; the unchanged historical prose does not itself establish a UTC
   241	bound. The changed sentences are rows 1–9 of A10.7.
   242	
   243	### A10.1.1 A conditional UTC assessment — RECOMMENDED (owner and Codex), BROADEN, AWAITING AUTHOR RULING
   244	
   245	Source: the decision memo, Part 1 (U1) and Part 3; its Codex review, F03, F04 and "Questions checked" 1; the
   246	tolerance evidence file. A10.1 stays as decided whatever the author rules here.
   247	
   248	**Notation.** h* is the receipt proof's selected inclusion height (A10.5). A2.1 counts it as confirmation one, so
   249	h*+5 is confirmation six. r(j) is the real (UTC) time at which block j was first produced.
   250	
   251	**Assumption A-UTC** (`assumption_id: "A-UTC-7200-6"`). *At least one of the six blocks h* … h*+5 has a header
   252	time no more than 7200 s before it was actually mined*, that is, `nTime(j) ≥ r(j) − 7200 s` for some j in
   253	[h*, h*+5]. A-UTC is an assumption of the UTC assessment only. It is not a Bitcoin consensus rule, the verifier
   254	does not check it, and no bundle carries evidence of it. It is a sufficient one-sided assumption, not a minimal one.
   255	Δ = 7200 s and m = 6 are frozen in the governing specification's catalog, not in the mutable trust file, and are
   256	not shown to be optimal.
   257	
   258	**Recommended claim text**, added after the A10.1 claim it qualifies (rows 20–21):
   259	
   260	> Under assumption A-UTC (at least one of the blocks h* … h*+5 has a header time no more than 7200 s before it was
   261	> actually mined; an assumption of the UTC assessment, not a consensus rule, and not checked by the verifier), the
   262	> bytes and both signatures existed before U = max nTime(h* … h*+5) + 7200 s, in UTC. V1 reports U only in
   263	> `utc_assessment`, beside its assumption identifier, outside the six stable fields. A2.1's refusal to bound the
   264	> observation lag B − C is unchanged.
   265	
   266	**Derivation** (memo, checked by Codex). (1) `A_sig` existed before r(h*), because h*'s merkle root commits to
   267	`SHA256(A_sig)` under the registered commitment and history assumptions. (2) r(h*) ≤ r(j) for every j ≥ h*,
   268	because block j commits to a chain that contains h*. (3) A-UTC supplies j in [h*, h*+5] with r(j) ≤ nTime(j) + 7200.
   269	(4) So r(h*) ≤ r(j) ≤ nTime(j) + 7200 ≤ U. The chain-order claims of A10.1 rest on steps 1–2 only.
   270	
   271	**Output contract** (memo, F03). `utc_assessment` contains `assumption_id: "A-UTC-7200-6"` and `utc_upper_bound`
   272	(RFC 3339 string or null). It is computed from the receipt proof's selected h* and its six qualified headers. Its
   273	bound is conditional on A-UTC and the existing commitment/history assumptions. It is null exactly when T is null.
   274	It does not change the six-field V1 stable result (A9:162, A9.4). Reporting a value with INVALID or UNVERIFIABLE
   275	does not establish a valid service receipt or valid service signatures. U is never a stable field, and V1's
   276	documentation and relying-party text never state U without A-UTC beside it.
   277	
   278	**Arithmetic and the 2106 boundary** (memo, F04). Verification of a preserved V1 receipt does not consult the
   279	current clock. Each bundled nTime is the profile's unsigned epoch-seconds value, widened before arithmetic:
   280	`U_seconds = 7200 + max(uint64(nTime(h*)), …, uint64(nTime(h*+5)))`, encoded as an RFC 3339 UTC string without
   281	reduction modulo 2^32 (for max nTime = 4294967295, U = `2106-02-07T08:28:15Z`, not a 1970 time). This 64-bit
   282	arithmetic covers preserved headers. Timestamp representation for Bitcoin blocks produced after the 2106 rollover
   283	of the 32-bit field is a Bitcoin protocol matter outside V1; the profile's serial-time unwrapping applies to DNSSEC
   284	RRSIG fields only.
   285	
   286	**What the signature does and does not do.** If the author adopts this section, his signature adopts A-UTC as a
   287	trust assumption. It does not establish that miners satisfy it, and nothing in V1 can.
   288	
   289	**Justification of the tolerance against the intended adversary**
   290	(`docs/decisions/2026-10-17-a-utc-tolerance-evidence.md`). The intended adversary is A9's backdater (A9:40): bytes
   291	that exist only at real time e, presented with a receipt whose U is earlier than e.
   292	- **Failure condition.** A-UTC fails, and U can be wrong, only if the miners of all six confirming blocks set
   293	  lagging timestamps: every block h* … h*+5 has a header time more than 7200 s before it was mined.
   294	- **A non-mining backdater** can choose only when to stamp and which proof to present. Lowest-height selection fixes
   295	  the result within a presented proof, but does not eliminate choice among available presentations. The UTC
   296	  assessment relies on A-UTC for the selected segment in every case. Over 559,718 retained complete six-block
   297	  windows (heights 369,715–941,944, 2015–2026; Bitcoin Block Arrival Time Dataset, multiple observers, CC0), every
   298	  window contained a block whose recorded arrival lag was at most 1,065 s. The minimum computed U minus the
   299	  inclusion block's recorded first-seen time was 6,135 s (median 9,908 s); U's UTC interpretation remains
   300	  conditional on A-UTC. If the selected observer readings do not understate production time, these observations
   301	  support A-UTC for the sampled windows. Observer clock error is not bounded here. The dataset's ±7200 s quality
   302	  filter and excluded incomplete windows prevent an inference about the frequency of A-UTC failures outside the
   303	  retained sample. The largest single-block lag observed was 3,918 s; the median was 18 s. A mempool.space sample of
   304	  481 blocks (2024) and Bowden et al. (arXiv:1801.07447, 2018) are consistent with this.
   305	- **Consensus.** Consensus requires each timestamp to exceed the median of the preceding eleven header times. The
   306	  evidence reproduces 545 windows in which every recorded first-seen-minus-MTP value exceeds 7200 s. These MTPs use
   307	  the actual predecessor timestamps and the observer-clock qualification. The count does not bound a coalition
   308	  changing the six timestamps, since changes within the window also change later MTPs. A majority coalition can
   309	  sustain lagging timestamps; A-UTC is not consensus protection against that adversary.
   310	- **Limits of the evidence.** The dataset's own quality check rejects any arrival more than 7200 s from its header
   311	  time, so it cannot show a lag above the threshold; its distribution below the threshold has no observation
   312	  between 3,918 s and 7,200 s. Observer clock error is not separated. The data describe ordinary miner behaviour,
   313	  not a deliberate coalition.
   314	- Δ equals Bitcoin's node-local forward tolerance; that match does not validate it. m = 6 reuses the k = 6 headers
   315	  that V1 already requires and pins, so U needs no new input.
   316	
   317	**Relation to A2.1 and guarantee 2.** A2.1 refuses to bound B − C, the service's observation lag, and keeps
   318	SLA-style bounds out of the assumption set. A-UTC bounds a different quantity (production time versus header time),
   319	and U never enters a verdict, the shipping predicate or S. Guarantee 2 keeps its chain-time meaning and is not
   320	translated to UTC (row 23).
   321	
   322	**Recorded, not obligations.** RFC 3161 tokens over `SHA256(A_sig)` from at least two independently operated
   323	time-stamp authorities (U_TSA = max_i(genTime_i + a_i), memo U2) are a recorded candidate, with no V2 or later
   324	obligation. Unresolved use requirements, also recorded only: whether a court or another examiner will accept A-UTC;
   325	whether a particular use needs qualified timestamps.
   326	
   327	The changed sentences are rows 20–24 of A10.7.
   328	
   329	## A10.2 The window establishes two comparisons, not issuance "inside" it — OWNER DECISION, NARROW-CLAIM (review A02)
   330	
   331	A9's opening says the keys "were allowed to issue during a stated window" (:36), and guarantee 2's heading says
   332	"Signing capability mints only inside the window, in chain time" (:441). The C4 verifier rule (:419-421) checks
   333	only two comparisons, and says "T itself is not compared with not-before".
   334	
   335	**Reading adopted.** The verifier establishes exactly two inclusive comparisons, with no margin:
   336	- the declared issue time is not earlier than `issuance-not-before`;
   337	- the verified chain-time T is not later than `issuance-not-after`.
   338	
   339	It does not establish that T is not earlier than not-before, that the declared issue time is not later than
   340	not-after, or that any signing act occurred inside the interval. The review's two arithmetic witnesses satisfy the
   341	two window comparisons and all three A2 temporal comparisons. They are not executed receipt vectors or evidence
   342	that an honest issuer ships those values. The checks do not change. The issuer invariant (:422, an attempt whose T
   343	would exceed not-after is not shipped) stays an issuer duty, not a verifier comparison. "Issuance window" stays the
   344	name of the record's two values. The changed sentences are rows 10–12 of A10.7.
   345	
   346	## A10.3 Reorganization depth, at k = 6 and `DepthK = k − 1` — OWNER DECISION, NARROW-CLAIM (review A03)
   347	
   348	A9 excludes "Bitcoin history deeper than k" (:49-50) and calls "Reorganization deeper than k" a residual (C5,
   349	:144). A9 also imports k = 6 under `DepthK = k − 1` (:105) and A2.1's count, in which the including block is the
   350	first confirmation.
   351	
   352	**Definition.** The depth of a reorganization is the number of blocks it removes from the tip of the chain. A
   353	reorganization of depth d removes every block that has d or fewer confirmations when it occurs. At k = 6
   354	confirmations, five blocks follow the anchor block; P5c's `depth` counts those five (`depth = c − 1`, so
   355	`DepthK = 5`).
   356	
   357	**Correction.** The registered assumption is that anchors with k or more confirmations are permanent (A1.6:
   358	"anchors at depth ≥ k are treated as permanent"). P5c's `Reorg` action can orphan an anchor only while
   359	`depth < DepthK`, that is, at five or fewer confirmations. So a reorganization of depth exactly k, which removes an
   360	anchor with k confirmations, is outside both. A9's "deeper than k" read literally leaves that case inside the
   361	claim, with no evidence. The excluded residual is therefore **a reorganization of depth k or more**. This narrows
   362	A9's literal prose claim at equality. It changes neither the model nor its permanence assumption, and withdraws no
   363	checked model result. This does not affect C4's chain-order gap (`g + 5 < r`), which the review found correct. The
   364	changed sentences are rows 13–14 of A10.7.
   365	
   366	## A10.4 Authority capture: honest signed material protected; publication remains an issuer duty; key substitution — RECOMMENDED (owner; option (e) also Codex), NARROW-CLAIM and, under option (e), BROADEN, AWAITING AUTHOR RULING (review A04)
   367	
   368	A9's adversary sentence includes "anyone who captures authority-channel credentials while signing capability stays
   369	uncompromised (A8.3's sense of capture)" (:41-42). It names no set of receipts and no object for "signing
   370	capability". A8.3 (:72-76) bounds the tested property: under capture, "a captor cannot reattribute honest frames,
   371	alter honest signer sets, or carry honest signatures onto other bytes or keys". That property concerns honest
   372	service material, not only receipts issued before disclosure; the capture fixtures disclose the credentials before
   373	the honest signing processes run. A8.5 (:123-124) puts "preventing a captured or merged authority from issuing new
   374	attestations" out of scope. A captor holding both required channel credentials can sign and anchor a key assertion
   375	that names its own keys, without publishing it; receipts under those keys can then pass V1. Capture of only one
   376	required channel does not generally suffice.
   377	
   378	**The author's standard** (2026-10-08, `docs/guarantee-tiers-DRAFT.md`, "Authority capture"): *"perhaps the right
   379	way to attack the problem is distribution and admission that governance changes are out of scope"*, and
   380	*"Authority capture must be visible in such instances. For an adjudicator to be convinced incorrectly requires the
   381	takeover be invisible."* The owner summarizes it as: prevent, no; rewrite the past, no; visible, yes. The summary
   382	is the owner's, not a quotation. These words are the motivation for A10.4. They are not evidence that the visibility
   383	property is established. No option below establishes it: option (e) prevents acceptance by a scoped set of relying
   384	parties; it does not ensure that anyone detects or publicly exposes a capture.
   385	
   386	**Recommended text.** Replace the adversary clause with:
   387	
   388	> anyone who captures authority-channel credentials (the DNS credential or the repository key, including both
   389	> together) while the original service keys stay secret and their signing capability stays uncompromised (A8.3's
   390	> sense of capture), as to frames honestly signed by those service keys, whether before or after credential
   391	> disclosure: such a captor cannot reattribute those frames to other keys or another service, alter their honest
   392	> signer sets, or carry their signatures onto other bytes or keys (A8.3);
   393	
   394	and add to "The boundaries", before its last sentence:
   395	
   396	> V1 does not prevent a captor of both required authority-channel credentials from issuing new receipts under a key
   397	> assertion that names the captor's own keys, even while the original service keys remain uncompromised. Public
   398	> publication of the key-assertion repository remains an issuer duty (A9.7, "Changed in effect"). If the
   399	> substituted assertion is publicly published, a third party can inspect the substituted keys. The verifier does
   400	> not check publication; V1 does not guarantee that a captor publishes the assertion, that capture is detected, or
   401	> that new receipts are publicly visible.
   402	
   403	Under option (e), row 26 replaces this boundary's first sentence with a scoped one (A10.4.1).
   404	
   405	Backdating stays with the temporal checks (C4's chain-order pre-commitment, C5) under their own assumptions; A8.3's
   406	runs do not test it (A8.3). Whole-author-sphere capture stays excluded (R-2.15). Guarantee 2's proviso
   407	"provided the authority channels are not also captured" (:443) is unchanged and consistent with this text.
   408	
   409	**Options for the author.** (a) Adopt the bounded honest-material claim above, with the new-receipt limitation and
   410	conditional publication statement. (b) Deliberately restrict even that claim to receipts honestly issued before
   411	capture; this gives up the honest post-capture material covered by A8.3's stated property and must be recorded as
   412	an additional author choice. (c) Extend presentation protection to arbitrary new receipts under captor-selected
   413	keys for every relying party, with issuer detection or public visibility; this needs a mechanism V1 does not have
   414	(for example a witnessed public log), and is not supported by this amendment. (d) Rule other text. **(e) Adopt (a)
   415	and add protection against key substitution: the trust-file service-key listing of A10.4.1**, with its ordered
   416	classifier and authorization closure, scoped to relying parties using an authentic, trustworthy release. This is a
   417	BROADEN; it adds mechanism (rows 25–33). Rows 15–16 take effect on (a) or (e); rows 25–33 only on (e).
   418	
   419	**Owner recommendation: (e)**, which the Codex review of the decision memo supports. If the author picks (e) and the
   420	listing is later cut for schedule reasons while A9.5's independent release boundary is established, (a) applies
   421	and the absence of new-key protection is stated (row 33). Release-key custody (A10.4.2) is a separate ruling and
   422	applies under every option. *The earlier draft's recommendation, (a) with option (c)'s mechanism registered as a
   423	named V2 obligation, is withdrawn.* Timestamp authorities and a witnessed append-only log of key assertions are
   424	recorded as **candidates only**; neither creates an obligation in V1, V2 or later.
   425	
   426	### A10.4.1 Protection against key substitution (option (e)) — RECOMMENDED (owner and Codex), BROADEN, AWAITING AUTHOR RULING
   427	
   428	Source: the decision memo, Part 2 and Part 3; its Codex review, F05, F07 and "Questions checked" 2–3. Mechanism
   429	details are the memo's.
   430	
   431	**Mechanism.** The trust configuration gains `service_keys`, an array of
   432	`{domain, kms_fp, gpg_fp, not_before, not_after, sources}` entries. Endpoints are finite, with
   433	not_before ≤ not_after. Fingerprints are canonical; entries are unique; the array has an explicit sort order.
   434	
   435	**Ordered classifier** (one new check; one new reason code, `SERVICE_KEYS_NOT_LISTED`; `TRUST_HORIZON` exists):
   436	
   437	> First, an exact match on D, both canonical fingerprints and both inclusive window endpoints passes this check.
   438	> Otherwise, overlap with any listed window **or** not_before ≤ as_of yields INVALID / SERVICE_KEYS_NOT_LISTED.
   439	> Otherwise yield UNVERIFIABLE / TRUST_HORIZON. Overlap includes a shared endpoint. Overall verdict and reason use
   440	> the registered failure precedence, not an early return from this check.
   441	
   442	**Authorization closure:**
   443	
   444	> The initial release independently authorizes and records the complete service-key schedule through as_of. Later
   445	> releases retain every existing entry unchanged, introduce no overlapping interval, and add an entry only if its
   446	> not_before is strictly greater than the previous as_of. Thus absence through a past horizon is permanently
   447	> closed. Completeness concerns authorized entries, not arbitrary channel assertions. The release authority must
   448	> not authorize a new service-key tuple solely because the two potentially captured channels assert it.
   449	
   450	**Issuer invariant.** No receipt ships under keys that are not listed in a published release.
   451	
   452	**Verdict stability.** Passes and both INVALID conclusions of this check survive every allowed append. An
   453	UNVERIFIABLE result may later pass, as A9.5 permits. This is local to this check: A9.4's contract stays identical
   454	output for identical declared inputs, the configuration included, and pin extensions can still change selection,
   455	T and U across releases.
   456	
   457	**V1's single window.** A9.6 registers one window. V1's configuration lists exactly one entry, authorized at the
   458	freeze. A successor window needs a release that lists it, authorized before it opens, in addition to A9.6's
   459	rotation registration.
   460	
   461	**Scope.** The protection covers relying parties who run an authentic verifier release whose trust configuration
   462	lists D's service keys. "Authentic" means authenticated distribution plus trustworthy release-authority behaviour;
   463	uncompromised key storage alone does not validate an authorization decision. Within this scope, a captor of both
   464	channel credentials who lacks release authorization cannot make a receipt under keys other than those listed pass
   465	V1. Such a receipt is INVALID when its window overlaps a listed window or starts at or before the configuration's
   466	`as_of`, and UNVERIFIABLE otherwise.
   467	
   468	**Limits.**
   469	- It prevents acceptance by those relying parties. It does not ensure that anyone detects or publicly exposes a
   470	  capture. It provides no issuer detection and no public visibility.
   471	- A horizon result means the keys are not yet covered. It is not proof of a takeover.
   472	- Release-key compromise is distribution compromise, already a stated boundary. A captor with both channels and the
   473	  release key can ship a release that lists its own keys. A release delivered to one party is not detectable by
   474	  others.
   475	- A stale trust file gives an honest new-window receipt `UNVERIFIABLE / TRUST_HORIZON`, a safe false reject until
   476	  the relying party updates. This cannot occur in V1, which has one window.
   477	- A8.3 and A8.5 supply no evidence for this property, and no formal model run supports it. Its evidence is the
   478	  check's construction plus prospective vectors (memo, Part 3, row 5), which are exit evidence once registered.
   479	
   480	**Change accounting.** This is a BROADEN, not a wording correction. C4 gains a third authenticated source for the
   481	key set, the release. The adversary text gains a protection, within the scope above. The scenario mapping's
   482	verdicts are unchanged; a fresh captor-key receipt is deliberately a newly rejected case. The signed bytes, the P8
   483	encoding, `A_sig`, the existing two-channel checks and every existing reason code are unchanged. The specification
   484	hash changes. Adding the listing to the cut order is a gate and scope change and is labelled as one (row 33). The
   485	changed sentences are rows 25–33 of A10.7.
   486	
   487	### A10.4.2 Release-key custody — RECOMMENDED (owner and Codex; gate timing by owner proposal, 2026-10-17), GATE, AWAITING AUTHOR RULING
   488	
   489	Source: the decision memo, Part 2 ("Release independence"); its Codex review, F05; `docs/V1-PROFILE.md` §5 and
   490	§14.2 items 1–2; the custody review `docs/reviews/custody/CODEX-REVIEW.md` (C01 and C02, HIGH); the owner's timing
   491	proposal of 2026-10-17, recorded here. This ruling applies under every option of A10.4.
   492	
   493	- **Prerequisite.** Independence of the release key from the DNS credential, the repository key and the issuing AWS
   494	  account is an existing prerequisite of A9.5's trust-configuration authority (A9:326-332). It already protects
   495	  V1's KSKs, repository trust and header pins against the channel captor, with or without the listing. It is still
   496	  OPEN (profile §14.2 item 1; YubiKey 2 is not in hand).
   497	- **Custody form.** Hardware or offline-software custody of the release key is acceptable **only** with a written
   498	  custody and release-authorization procedure. The procedure states who authorizes releases, where signing occurs,
   499	  and how backups stay outside the operational environment of the service. After the custody review it must also
   500	  state (C01) the independent review-and-build environment that checks each candidate release against an approved
   501	  baseline kept outside the operational environment and binds approval to the exact archive hash, and (C02) the
   502	  controlled media crossing into and out of the signing session. The dated custody record names the procedure.
   503	- **Hardware is not independence.** Buying hardware alone does not establish independence.
   504	- **When: the first public verifier release, not the freeze.** The custody review found that real independence
   505	  needs an independent review-and-sign environment and a controlled media crossing (C01, C02), which cannot be ready
   506	  by 30 Oct. The *first public verifier release* is the first verifier release signed by the production release key
   507	  and offered to relying parties.
   508	  - **Frozen at the 30 Oct freeze:** this requirement; the release format (the release archive, the release-signature
   509	    format, and how the trust-file hash binds to the release; profile §14.2 item 2), which must be frozen by 30 Oct;
   510	    and the custody procedure's required contents (the bullet above).
   511	  - **After the freeze, before the first public verifier release** (target: before the 20 Nov issuance milestone):
   512	    the environment is built and rehearsed, the written procedure is reviewed, and independence is established.
   513	- **Test releases.** Every verifier release made before that gate is signed with a separate TEST release key that no
   514	  production trust anchor accepts. Its public key is never published as the release key, never named in the freeze
   515	  record or relying-party text, and never introduced by a production release. So no pre-gate release can reach a
   516	  relying party as a real release. (Profile §3.5's TEST release provisioning is consistent with this.)
   517	- **Risks and mitigations** (owner's proposal):
   518	  1. *Schedule.* The schedule risk moves later. Mitigation: start preparing the environment and procedure early.
   519	  2. *Post-freeze specification change.* Avoided: the release format is frozen by 30 Oct.
   520	  3. *Convenience release* (a release signed outside the procedure because the environment is not ready). Closed by
   521	     the test-release rule above.
   522	  4. *The author's time in November.* Accepted by the author, 2026-10-17: *"(4) is acceptable to me"*. This
   523	     accepts that risk only; it is not his ruling on A10.4.2.
   524	  5. *Relying parties.* No risk to them from the move: nothing is public before the gate.
   525	- **Consequence for the profile.** Profile §5 puts the release public key's fingerprint in the freeze record. Under
   526	  this timing the production release key need not exist at the freeze, so its fingerprint is recorded with the
   527	  first public verifier release's bootstrap publication instead. That change, and moving profile §14.2 item 1's
   528	  deadline from 30 Oct to this gate, follow only after the author's ruling.
   529	- **Fallback.** If independence cannot be established before the first public verifier release, the author receives
   530	  a NARROW-MODEL of the channel-capture claim (A9:41-42, as amended by rows 15 and 25) before that release. Cutting
   531	  the listing does not cure it. A custody failure is never an automatic, invisible reversion of the threat model.
   532	
   533	The changed sentences are rows 34–36 of A10.7.
   534	
   535	## A10.5 Attestation selection, in stages — OWNER DECISION, CLARIFY (review A05)
   536	
   537	C5 selects "the verified attestation at the lowest block height in the presented OTS proof" (:144). C6 says "A
   538	selected anchor (C5) beyond the newest pin is `UNVERIFIABLE`", "Every supplied header must be pinned" and "An
   539	unselected attestation without its headers supplies no fact and is not a failure" (:145). "Verified" can be read
   540	with or without pin coverage. The profile already fixes one order (`docs/V1-PROFILE.md` §7.1–§7.2, OWNER
   541	2026-10-14, G05, G07, G23; precedence rows 10–11). This amendment states that order as A9's meaning.
   542	
   543	These stages explain qualification and selection within the existing verifier. They do not replace other
   544	registered checks, their dependencies, or dispatch and reason-code precedence. A failure of an independently
   545	performable required check takes precedence over an unperformable check under A4.2. The header-pool link checks,
   546	OTS grammar and stamp checks, and selected-height transaction/path checks remain unchanged. A stage's UNVERIFIABLE
   547	entry is an unperformable-check result, not an early return that suppresses other checks.
   548	
   549	**Stages**, for each of the receipt, snapshot and git proofs:
   550	1. **Header pool.** Every supplied header, used or not, is checked against the pins. A header at a height with no
   551	   pin gives `UNVERIFIABLE / HEADERS_NOT_COVERED`; a header that differs from its pin gives `INVALID /
   552	   HEADER_CONFLICTS_PIN`.
   553	2. **Qualification ("verified").** A Bitcoin attestation at height h qualifies only if the bundled header at h has
   554	   a merkle root equal to the attestation message, headers h … h+5 are all bundled, each equals its pin, and each
   555	   links to the one before. Pin coverage is part of "verified". A pinned header at h whose merkle root contradicts
   556	   the attestation gives `INVALID / OTS_MERKLE_MISMATCH`.
   557	3. **Selection.** The selected anchor is the qualifying attestation at the lowest height h*. T and the confirmation
   558	   count come from h*. A non-qualifying attestation, including one without headers or one beyond the newest pin,
   559	   is never selected; it supplies no fact and is not a failed check, except as stages 1–2 state. A failed check at
   560	   h* never moves selection to a higher height.
   561	4. **No qualifying attestation.** The check is unperformable (`HEADERS_MISSING`, or `ANCHOR_PENDING_ONLY` if only
   562	   pending attestations; `SNAPSHOT_NOT_ANCHORED` and `GIT_NOT_ANCHORED` for those proofs). The overall verdict is
   563	   `UNVERIFIABLE` unless another independently performable required check fails, in which case it is `INVALID`
   564	   under A4.2.
   565	
   566	**Cases**, each subject to A4.2 precedence over any other failed check. A qualifying path at h = 100 and a lower,
   567	headerless attestation at h = 90: h* = 100. A sole attestation beyond the newest pin, without headers: stage 4,
   568	unperformable. The same with its headers supplied: stage 1, `HEADERS_NOT_COVERED`, unperformable. So C6's
   569	"selected anchor beyond the newest pin" cannot arise; an anchor beyond the pins makes a check unperformable through
   570	stage 1 or stage 4. The assembled C5 claim cell is in A10.1. The changed sentences are rows 17–18 of A10.7.
   571	
